------------------------------------------------------------
Development Philosophy
------------------------------------------------------------

Performance is a primary feature.

Architecture is designed before implementation.

The application shall remain focused on one task:

Fast inspection of scientific microscopy images.

The software is educational.

The source code should teach Object Pascal and software design,
not merely implement functionality.

------------------------------------------------------------
Programming Manifesto
------------------------------------------------------------

1. Performance is a feature.

2. Architecture before implementation.

3. One responsibility per class.

4. Code should teach.

5. Comments explain WHY.

6. Mouse is the primary interface.

7. Benchmark before optimizing.

8. Every commit must compile.

9. Every important decision is documented.

10. MView remains an image viewer,
    not a file manager.

Guiding motto

Simple classes.
Fast execution.
Clear architecture.

------------------------------------------------------------
Coding Standard
------------------------------------------------------------

Indentation

2 spaces

Tabs

Never

Line width

120 characters

begin

Always on a separate line

Declarations

One declaration per line

Naming

Units

uNavigator
uRenderer
uImageLoader

Classes

TNavigator
TRenderer

Fields

FCurrentImage

Properties

CurrentImage

Methods

LoadImage
NextImage
PreviousDirectory

------------------------------------------------------------
Commenting Philosophy
------------------------------------------------------------

Every unit begins with a descriptive header.

Each class explains

- purpose
- responsibilities
- non-responsibilities

Comments explain

WHY

rather than

HOW

Important design decisions should be documented both

inside the code

and

inside DecisionLog.md

Teaching comments are encouraged.

The code base should be understandable by programmers
learning Object Pascal.

------------------------------------------------------------
Architectural Decisions
------------------------------------------------------------

No image database.

Recursive directory scanning.

Internal directory tree.

Thumbnail cache only.

Mouse scripting remains an independent subsystem.

Commands remain independent of user input.

The viewer is command-driven.

------------------------------------------------------------
Workflow Decisions
------------------------------------------------------------

Primary workflow

Total Commander

↓

Select experiment

↓

Open in MView

↓

Rapid image inspection

↓

Move interesting images

↓

Return to Total Commander

MView complements file managers.

It does not replace them.

------------------------------------------------------------
User Interface Philosophy
------------------------------------------------------------

No menu bar.

No toolbar.

No permanent controls.

Fullscreen image presentation.

Right-click context menu initially contains only

Exit

The image remains the central focus.

------------------------------------------------------------
Mouse Philosophy
------------------------------------------------------------

Primary browsing quadrant

Bottom Left

Sort

Date (Newest → Oldest)

Secondary browsing quadrant

Top Right

Sort

Filename (Natural)

Inspection quadrant

Bottom Right

Zoom

Upper Left

Reserved

Default gestures

Gesture Left

Previous Directory

Gesture Right

Next Directory

Gesture Down

Exit

Quadrants represent workflow contexts rather than
simple screen regions.

------------------------------------------------------------
Performance Philosophy
------------------------------------------------------------

Investigate

Fast loading

Aggressive preloading

Directional preload

Cache management

Bitmap reuse

Rendering performance

Performance hypotheses will be verified by benchmark
programs before implementation.

